## 10. Anti-Patterns Checklist

This section documents common mistakes and anti-patterns specific to TypeScript/React/Next.js that should be flagged during code review as **SHOULD FIX (Critical Best Practices)** with appropriate severity levels.

### 10.1 Performance Anti-Patterns

#### Synchronous Blocking Operations in Hot Paths [HIGH]

**Problem**: localStorage/sessionStorage writes on every state change block the main thread.

```typescript
// ❌ Bad - writes on every keystroke
useEffect(() => {  
  localStorage.setItem('state', JSON.stringify(state))
}, [state])

// ✅ Good - debounced writes (500ms delay)
useEffect(() => {  
  const timeoutId = setTimeout(() => {
    localStorage.setItem('state', JSON.stringify(state))
  }, 500)
  return () => clearTimeout(timeoutId)
}, [state])

// ✅ Alternative - save only on significant events
useEffect(() => {  
  localStorage.setItem('state', JSON.stringify(state))
}, [state.currentStep]) // Only save when step changes, not on text input
```

**Impact**: Degrades UX, especially on slower devices. Each write can take 1-5ms, blocking user interactions.

---

#### Missing Memoization for Expensive Operations [MEDIUM]

**Problem**: Expensive computations re-run on every render.

```typescript
// ❌ Bad - recalculates on every render
function Component({ items }: { items: Item[] }) {
  const sortedItems = items
    .map(item => ({ ...item, score: calculateComplexScore(item) }))
    .sort((a, b) => b.score - a.score)
  
  return <List data={sortedItems} />
}

// ✅ Good - memoized
function Component({ items }: { items: Item[] }) {
  const sortedItems = useMemo(
    () => items
      .map(item => ({ ...item, score: calculateComplexScore(item) }))
      .sort((a, b) => b.score - a.score),
    [items]
  )
  
  return <List data={sortedItems} />
}
```

**When to use**: Operations taking >5ms, array transformations with >100 items, recursive calculations.

---

#### Unbounded List Rendering [HIGH]

**Problem**: Rendering large lists (>100 items) without virtualization.

```typescript
// ❌ Bad - renders all 10,000 items
{items.map(item => <ItemCard key={item.id} {...item} />)}

// ✅ Good - virtualized rendering (react-window)
import { FixedSizeList } from 'react-window'

<FixedSizeList
  height={600}
  itemCount={items.length}
  itemSize={80}
  width="100%"
>
  {({ index, style }) => (
    <div style={style}>
      <ItemCard {...items[index]} />
    </div>
  )}
</FixedSizeList>
```

**Threshold**: Use virtualization for lists with >100-200 items, or if item rendering is expensive.

---

#### Multiple Sequential API Calls (Waterfall) [MEDIUM]

**Problem**: Dependent API calls that could be parallelized.

```typescript
// ❌ Bad - sequential (3 seconds total if each takes 1s)
const user = await fetchUser(id)
const posts = await fetchPosts(user.id)
const comments = await fetchComments(user.id)

// ✅ Good - parallel (1 second total)
const [user, posts, comments] = await Promise.all([
  fetchUser(id),
  fetchPosts(id),  // If posts/comments don't depend on user data
  fetchComments(id)
])
```

**Note**: Only parallelize truly independent calls. Maintain sequential calls when data dependencies exist.

---

#### Missing Dependencies in useEffect [HIGH]

**Problem**: useEffect with incomplete dependency array causes stale closures and bugs.

```typescript
// ❌ Bad - userId changes not tracked
useEffect(() => {
  fetchData(userId)
}, []) // Missing userId dependency!

// ✅ Good - complete dependencies
useEffect(() => {
  fetchData(userId)
}, [userId])

// ✅ Alternative - if callback needs to be stable
const fetchDataCallback = useCallback(() => {
  fetchData(userId)
}, [userId])

useEffect(() => {
  fetchDataCallback()
}, [fetchDataCallback])
```

**Rule**: Always include ALL external values used inside useEffect. Use ESLint rule `react-hooks/exhaustive-deps`.

---

### 10.2 React/Next.js Anti-Patterns

#### Missing Error Boundaries [HIGH]

**Problem**: Async operations or external data fetching without error boundaries allow entire app to crash.

```typescript
// ❌ Bad - no error boundary
export default function Page() {
  return <ComponentThatMightThrow />
}

// ✅ Good - wrapped in error boundary
import { ErrorBoundary } from '@/components/error-boundary'

export default function Page() {
  return (
    <ErrorBoundary fallback={<ErrorFallback />}>
      <ComponentThatMightThrow />
    </ErrorBoundary>
  )
}
```

**Where to add**: Around async data fetching, third-party components, dynamic imports, user-generated content.

**Reference**: See Section 7.1 for error boundary implementation patterns.

---

#### Client-Side Data Fetching When Server Components Would Work [MEDIUM]

**Problem**: Unnecessarily shipping data fetching logic to the client.

```typescript
// ❌ Bad - client-side fetch for static data
'use client'

export default function Page() {
  const [data, setData] = useState(null)
  
  useEffect(() => {
    fetch('/api/data')
      .then(r => r.json())
      .then(setData)
  }, [])
  
  if (!data) return <Loading />
  return <div>{data.title}</div>
}

// ✅ Good - server component with async fetch
export default async function Page() {
  const data = await fetch('/api/data', {
    next: { revalidate: 3600 } // Cache for 1 hour
  }).then(r => r.json())
  
  return <div>{data.title}</div>
}
```

**When to use client**: User interactions, browser APIs (localStorage, geolocation), real-time updates, client-specific state.

**Reference**: See Section 3.2 for Server vs Client Component guidance.

---

#### Using Index as Key for Dynamic Lists [MEDIUM]

**Problem**: Index keys break React reconciliation when list order changes.

```typescript
// ❌ Bad - index as key (breaks on reorder/filter)
{items.map((item, index) => (
  <Item key={index} {...item} />
))}

// ✅ Good - stable unique ID
{items.map(item => (
  <Item key={item.id} {...item} />
))}

// ✅ Acceptable - if list never reorders and items are primitives
{staticList.map((label, index) => (
  <span key={`label-${index}`}>{label}</span>
))}
```

**Rule**: Only use index as key if: list is static, items never reorder/filter, and items are primitives.

---

#### Prop Drilling Instead of Context [LOW]

**Problem**: Passing props through 3+ levels of components.

```typescript
// ❌ Bad - prop drilling through 4 levels
<App user={user}>
  <Layout user={user}>
    <Page user={user}>
      <Component user={user} />

// ✅ Good - context for widely-used data
const UserContext = createContext<User | null>(null)

function App() {
  return (
    <UserContext.Provider value={user}>
      <Layout>
        <Page>
          <Component /> {/* useContext(UserContext) internally */}
```

**Threshold**: Use Context when passing data through 3+ levels, or data is needed by many unrelated components.

---

### 10.3 Security Anti-Patterns

#### PII or Secrets in localStorage Without Encryption [HIGH]

**Problem**: Sensitive data stored in plaintext is vulnerable to XSS attacks.

```typescript
// ❌ Bad - plaintext sensitive data
localStorage.setItem('user', JSON.stringify({ 
  ssn: '123-45-6789',
  creditCard: '4111-1111-1111-1111'
}))

// ✅ Good - don't store PII client-side
// Store in secure HttpOnly cookie server-side instead

// ✅ Acceptable - if encryption is required client-side
import { encrypt, decrypt } from '@/lib/crypto'
const encrypted = encrypt(JSON.stringify(userData))
localStorage.setItem('user', encrypted)
```

**Rule**: Never store SSN, credit cards, passwords, or other PII in localStorage/sessionStorage. Use secure HttpOnly cookies for sensitive session data.

---

#### Unvalidated User Input in Dynamic Content [HIGH]

**Problem**: XSS vulnerability when rendering user-generated content.

```typescript
// ❌ Bad - XSS vulnerability
<div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: userComment }} />

// ✅ Good - sanitized input
import DOMPurify from 'dompurify'

<div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ 
  __html: DOMPurify.sanitize(userComment, {
    ALLOWED_TAGS: ['b', 'i', 'em', 'strong', 'a'],
    ALLOWED_ATTR: ['href']
  }) 
}} />

// ✅ Better - avoid dangerouslySetInnerHTML entirely
<div>{userComment}</div> // React auto-escapes
```

**Rule**: Always sanitize user input before rendering as HTML. Prefer React's automatic escaping when possible.

---

#### API Keys or Secrets in Client Code [HIGH]

**Problem**: Environment variables prefixed with `NEXT_PUBLIC_` are exposed to the client.

```typescript
// ❌ Bad - exposes secret to client
const apiKey = process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_API_SECRET

// ✅ Good - server-side only
// In API route or Server Component:
const apiKey = process.env.API_SECRET // No NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix
```

**Rule**: Never prefix secrets with `NEXT_PUBLIC_`. Use server-side API routes or Server Components for authenticated requests.

---

### 10.4 Accessibility Anti-Patterns

#### Interactive Elements Without Keyboard Support [HIGH]

**Problem**: Div/span used for interaction without keyboard accessibility.

```typescript
// ❌ Bad - no keyboard support
<div onClick={handleClick}>Click me</div>

// ✅ Good - use semantic button
<button onClick={handleClick}>Click me</button>

// ✅ Acceptable - if button styling doesn't work
<div 
  role="button"
  tabIndex={0}
  onClick={handleClick}
  onKeyDown={(e) => {
    if (e.key === 'Enter' || e.key === ' ') {
      e.preventDefault()
      handleClick()
    }
  }}
>
  Click me
</div>
```

**Rule**: Use semantic HTML (`<button>`, `<a>`) for interactive elements. If divs are required, add full keyboard support.

**Reference**: See `.cursor/rules/accessibility.mdc` Section 3 for comprehensive keyboard navigation patterns.

---

#### Missing ARIA Labels on Icon-Only Buttons [HIGH]

**Problem**: Screen readers can't identify button purpose.

```typescript
// ❌ Bad - screen reader announces "button" with no context
<button>
  <CloseIcon />
</button>

// ✅ Good - accessible label
<button aria-label="Close dialog">
  <CloseIcon aria-hidden="true" />
</button>

// ✅ Alternative - visible text with screen reader class
<button>
  <CloseIcon aria-hidden="true" />
  <span className="sr-only">Close dialog</span>
</button>
```

**Rule**: All icon-only interactive elements must have `aria-label` or visually hidden text.

---

#### Form Inputs Without Associated Labels [HIGH]

**Problem**: Screen readers can't identify input purpose; reduces click target.

```typescript
// ❌ Bad - no label association
<div>
  <span>Email</span>
  <input type="email" placeholder="Enter email" />
</div>

// ✅ Good - properly associated label
<div>
  <label htmlFor="email-input">Email</label>
  <input id="email-input" type="email" placeholder="Enter email" />
</div>

// ✅ Alternative - label wrapping input
<label>
  Email
  <input type="email" placeholder="Enter email" />
</label>
```

**Rule**: All form inputs must have associated `<label>` elements using `htmlFor`/`id` or wrapping.

**Reference**: See `.cursor/rules/accessibility.mdc` Section 9 for comprehensive form accessibility patterns.

---

### 10.5 TypeScript Anti-Patterns

#### Using `any` Type [HIGH]

**Problem**: Defeats TypeScript's type safety.

```typescript
// ❌ Bad - any type
function processData(data: any) {
  return data.value // No type checking
}

// ✅ Good - unknown with type narrowing
function processData(data: unknown) {
  if (typeof data === 'object' && data !== null && 'value' in data) {
    return (data as { value: string }).value
  }
  throw new Error('Invalid data structure')
}

// ✅ Better - proper typing
interface DataStructure {
  value: string
}

function processData(data: DataStructure) {
  return data.value
}
```

**Rule**: The `any` type is **forbidden** per Section 2.1. Use `unknown` with type narrowing instead.

---

#### Non-Null Assertions Without Validation [MEDIUM]

**Problem**: Using `!` operator without guaranteeing value exists.

```typescript
// ❌ Bad - assumes user exists
const userName = users.find(u => u.id === id)!.name // Runtime error if not found

// ✅ Good - handle undefined case
const user = users.find(u => u.id === id)
if (!user) throw new Error(`User ${id} not found`)
const userName = user.name

// ✅ Alternative - optional chaining with fallback
const userName = users.find(u => u.id === id)?.name ?? 'Unknown'
```

**Rule**: Only use non-null assertion (`!`) when TypeScript's type system can't infer a guarantee you know is true. Always prefer explicit validation.

